 ## Background
-The concept of tensor decomposition for radiance fields was introduced by [TensoRF](3026-tensorf.md), which demonstrated that radiance fields exhibit low-rank structures in both geometry and appearance [S1]. Prior methods like [NeRF](4972-neural-radiance-fields.md) relied on MLPs, which are memory-efficient but slow to train, while voxel-based methods like [Direct Voxel Grid Optimization](4694-direct-voxel-grid-optimization.md) were fast but memory-intensive [G]. TensoRF bridges this gap by using tensor factorization to achieve both speed and compactness [S1]. The joint optimization variant addresses the instability of optimizing poses with voxel-based representations by leveraging the spectral bias and smoothness properties of the tensor factors [11498-joint-optimization-of-camera-pose-and-decomposed-low-rank-te.md].
+The concept of tensor decomposition for radiance fields was introduced by [TensoRF](3026-tensorf.md), which demonstrated that radiance fields exhibit low-rank structures in both geometry and appearance. Prior methods like [NeRF](4972-neural-radiance-fields.md) relied on MLPs, which are memory-efficient but slow to train, while voxel-based methods like [Direct Voxel Grid Optimization](4694-direct-voxel-grid-optimization.md) were fast but memory-intensive [G]. TensoRF bridges this gap by using tensor factorization to achieve both speed and compactness [3026-tensorf.md]. The joint optimization variant addresses the instability of optimizing poses with voxel-based representations by leveraging the spectral bias and smoothness properties of the tensor factors [11498-joint-optimization-of-camera-pose-and-decomposed-low-rank-te.md].
 
